  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The manufacturing account collects the cost of making the goods and transfers the cost of production to the trading account.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Prime cost is the three direct costs; factory overhead is added afterwards.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    25,000 + 180,000 + 5,000 − 20,000 = ₦190,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is part of the cost of getting material into the factory, so it forms part of direct materials.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is the cost of delivering goods to customers, which is a selling expense of the period.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is incurred for the factory but cannot be traced to a unit, so it is an indirect factory cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The supervisor does not make a traceable unit; his salary is indirect factory labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The office is outside the factory, so its depreciation is an administration expense.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Opening work in progress was finished this year, so it is added; the closing figure is deducted.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    300,000 + 120,000 + 30,000 − 40,000 = ₦410,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    410,000 + 50,000 − 45,000 = ₦415,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Manufacturing profit is the transfer price less the cost of production, 240,000 − 200,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It varies directly with the units made and can be traced to them, so it is a direct expense.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The hire is incurred for a specific order and is traceable to it, unlike the general factory costs.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sales manager works after the factory gate; his salary is a selling and administration cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its output is the cost of production, which the trading account then adjusts for finished goods.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Goods made but not sold are stock, so they are deducted in arriving at cost of goods sold.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two work-in-progress adjustments must be included with the factory overhead.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Transfer value 5,000 × 12 = 60,000, less cost 50,000 = ₦10,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Scrap is a recovery of factory cost, so it reduces the cost of production by being credited to the account.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He supports production but does not make a traceable unit, so his wage is indirect factory labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Power is consumed by the factory as a whole and cannot be traced to one unit.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The plant serves the factory in general, so its depreciation is an indirect factory cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is incurred for the factory, so it belongs to the manufacturing account.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The accounts office is an administration cost, incurred after the goods are made.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Advertising is a selling expense of the period.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    150,000 − 60,000 − 40,000 = ₦50,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    120,000 + 80,000 + 10,000 = ₦210,000; the factory overhead is added later.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    260,000 − 50,000 − 20,000 + 30,000 = ₦220,000; reverse the ladder.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Closing WIP is deducted, so understating it deducts too little and overstates the cost of production.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The transfer price exceeds the cost of production, and the difference is the manufacturing profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The account isolates factory cost, so that the trading account shows the gross margin on goods made.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Gross profit is sales less cost of goods sold: 600,000 − 420,000 = ₦180,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cost of production is the cost of the goods that came out of the factory during the year.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    No single order caused it, so the premium is spread over all production as an overhead.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The operators work on the product and their time can be booked to it.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Packing for delivery happens after the factory gate and is part of selling and distribution.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Storage of completed goods is a distribution cost, not a cost of making them.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    60,000 + 25,000 + 35,000 + 40,000 = ₦160,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The plant is used in the factory, so its depreciation is a factory overhead; the other three are period costs.
  ]

]

